WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
Speaker 1: Hey, this might be interesting and&nbsp;
informative for you guys. As many of you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:12.000
we get five or so thousand messages a day&nbsp;
across all of our different platforms. So like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.560
YouTube comments, Facebook comments, emails,&nbsp;
Twitter replies, all these different things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.120
it's impossible to keep up with all of it. And&nbsp;
so we just can't. We just don't, OK? Every once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:29.120
in a while, I like to engage with someone. So&nbsp;
like six months ago, an anti-vax emailer piqued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.640
my interest and I said, Let me reply and let's see&nbsp;
what happens. And then I showed you that exchange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:42.080
and it was really, really wacky and disturbing. So&nbsp;
I did it again. I did it again. The other day, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.520
tweeted about the jobs numbers. Now, one of the&nbsp;
things we talked about on yesterday's bonus show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.920
is in the midst of everything going on&nbsp;
politically. The jobs numbers, which are very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:00.160
good. I mean, by every traditional measure, we're&nbsp;
basically at full employment now, many of you will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.840
say, but a lot of people are still paycheck to&nbsp;
paycheck and the wages aren't good and the jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.960
are low quality and b, oh, one hundred percent.&nbsp;
I'm with you all the way. I'm just saying that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:15.840
by traditional measures which Republicans love to&nbsp;
use when it's a Republican president, we are very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.360
close to what is considered full employment. And&nbsp;
last week, there were extraordinary jobs numbers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:28.080
Nearly 700000 new jobs were created in February.&nbsp;
We got the February numbers last week. These are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.480
very, very good numbers. And so I simply tweeted&nbsp;
just to see kind of, you know, feel around what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:38.960
going on. The jobs numbers are very compelling,&nbsp;
just really not a very controversial tweet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:49.360
And a guy named Andrew responded to me and said,&nbsp;
You mean all the jobs Biden destroyed with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:57.040
lockdown mania? Now, as you can probably imagine,&nbsp;
there's like three or four layers on which this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:06.320
is. This is very fallacious. We're talking about&nbsp;
job creation right now. Jobs were gaining jobs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:15.040
And of course, the job losses that we saw happened&nbsp;
under Donald Trump. And so the comment just it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.434
doesn't make any sense. But I said, Hey, here's an&nbsp;
opportunity to try to figure out where this person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.434 --> 00:02:25.280
is coming from. So the first thing was I wanted&nbsp;
to establish, does the person even know what I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:33.280
talking about? So I wrote back and I said, Did you&nbsp;
see the numbers today? Because they may not even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:38.320
know that I think it was six hundred and seventy&nbsp;
eight thousand jobs were created in February. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.360
might just be responding without having any&nbsp;
idea what it is that I'm talking about. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:50.000
Andrew wrote back and said, Yeah. Meaning I did&nbsp;
see the jobs numbers. You can't brag about job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:59.520
creation when your party caused all the job loss.&nbsp;
And again, bragging, we're just talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:08.000
what are the jobs numbers? Is it good to create&nbsp;
jobs? And if it is, if you blame who's in the Oval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:14.240
Office when jobs are lost, don't they get credit&nbsp;
when jobs are created now? You and I, hopefully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:19.600
all of us have a more nuanced understanding of job&nbsp;
creation, and we understand that to some degree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:27.040
on a delay, job creation or job loss partially&nbsp;
reflects the actions of the president,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:35.360
but to a significant degree has to do with global&nbsp;
factors, broader economic factors and and things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:39.120
that have nothing to do with the president. But&nbsp;
you've got to be able to play both sides if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:43.680
blame a president for job loss. You've got to&nbsp;
at least give some equivalent amount of credit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:49.920
for job creation. But this person is so confused.&nbsp;
You can't brag about job creation when your party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:58.400
caused the job loss. So I followed up and tried&nbsp;
to explain. I said quote almost all the job loss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:05.600
was in 2020, when Donald Trump was&nbsp;
president. What exactly are you referring to?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:15.280
To which Andrew responded, The needless policies&nbsp;
that were under Democrats via their governors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:22.320
and congressmen, they shut the country down&nbsp;
needlessly. It's been shown. Many times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:30.960
lockdown policies didn't help stop the spread,&nbsp;
but hurt devastated the economy. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.080
so a couple of different things. First of all,&nbsp;
this experiment was basically a waste of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:44.640
and what I mean by that is each response moved the&nbsp;
discussion to something different, and it didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:51.280
even seem to comprehend that time moves forward&nbsp;
as we understand it and who was president at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:57.520
different points in time. But it's also wrong on&nbsp;
some pretty basic facts. Number one, the economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.200
even by the end. I mean, listen, I'm glad to admit&nbsp;
it because again, it has. It has a little to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:09.440
with the president, but not that much. By the end&nbsp;
of Trump's presidency, the economy was looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:15.760
pretty OK. And it continued improving under Joe&nbsp;
Biden. We now have a very shaky stock market and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:21.120
inflation, and we have these global things. But&nbsp;
that trend, we were already seeing it under Trump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.720
and it continued under Biden. So even the claim&nbsp;
that everything that was done in 2020 hurt the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:35.280
economy irreversibly, it's just not true. But&nbsp;
maybe more importantly. We learned that it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.240
matter, even if I try to engage in good faith&nbsp;
and understand where this person is coming from.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:49.440
I posted about February jobs numbers. The guy&nbsp;
responded and talked about job losses from 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:55.600
but blamed Biden for them, even though Trump&nbsp;
was president, then changed the conversation to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:01.520
so-called democratic lockdowns. Even though it was&nbsp;
all under Trump's watch, I mean, it's red herring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:07.040
after red herring, after straw man after red&nbsp;
herring. So I think what I learned is this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:13.200
a waste of my time, and I don't know because&nbsp;
I don't know this Andrew Guy. I don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:17.920
if he's ignorant and maybe cognitively limited&nbsp;
in terms of being able to engage on the facts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.840
or whether he's arguing in bad faith or whether&nbsp;
he's just trolling me, or whether he was confused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:28.960
or whatever the case may be. I just don't&nbsp;
know, quite frankly. But needless to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.880
we didn't learn anything from that conversation,&nbsp;
and I've probably had my fill for at least a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:38.480
couple of months. But if I decide to do this&nbsp;
again, I'll come, I'll come back to you.

